    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d375">

                <pb facs="0528" n="454" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp02v01/pg454"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/7982</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-12-06T13:00:00+00:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">December 6, 1917, 1 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 4.58 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The President’s address to Congress<anchor n="3" xml:id="d375-f1"/> has been
                    received here by the whole press and public with the most enthusiastic approval
                    and <pb facs="0529" n="455" xml:id="pg455"/> satisfaction; except the
                    President’s speech which brought us into the war, it is regarded as his most
                    important utterance. Considerable satisfaction is felt at his forecast of the
                    possibility in certain circumstances of Germany’s exclusion from the free
                    economic intercourse which must inevitably spring out of the partnership of a
                    real peace; concerning this point I sent with my despatch No. 7615 of December
                        3<anchor n="1" xml:id="d375-f2"/> a more important memorandum which please
                    transmit to the President as soon as received.</p>
                <p>There is also much gratification at his recommendation of war against Austria and
                    some minor regret is expressed that he did not mention also Bulgaria and
                    Turkey.</p>
                <p>The whole effect of the speech is greatly to cheer up and hearten the British and
                    to put the President still more clearly, if that were possible, as the
                    preeminent leader in the war.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Page</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
